# Service registry: what name maps to which port.
#
# Applications name a service and never handle a port number. This file is the
# translation, and because sync pushes one tree to every computer it is
# effectively global by construction -- there is nothing to keep in step.
#
# Ports below 100 are reserved for OS services and cannot be bound by an
# application, so user services start at 100.
#
#   name        port

chat            100
echo            101

# Manufacturer node (spec/manufacturer-controller.md, spec/node-software.md).
# Both are marked encrypted with `netadmin encrypt`.
mfg-control     110
mfg-report      111

# 112 and 113 are FREE. They held `mfg-enroll` and `icmp-echo`, and neither was ever
# resolved by name:
#
#   enrollment runs before the turtle has been admitted, so it has no session and no
#   route -- it rides acknet with its own protocol name and its own MAC
#   (scada-design.md R9.11a), never a routed service port;
#   icmp is a layer of its own and dispatches on a message field, not a port.
#
# Both were registered on the assumption they would be needed and never were. Left
# free deliberately rather than backfilled, so the next service takes a number nobody
# has old policy for -- a stale `/net/ports` entry marking 112 encrypted would refuse
# plaintext on whatever landed there next.

# Supervisory and contract traffic. TWO services rather than one, deliberately
# (mfg/controller/orders.lua): they answer to different actors.
#
#   mfg-scada   a local OPERATOR at an HMI -- supervisory actions on our own plant
#   mfg-order   a STATION across the boundary -- the contract between two ledgers
#
# Collapsing them would give a station an operator's reach into the queue. Both
# should be encrypted on BOTH ends (`netadmin encrypt mfg-scada`); a port marked
# encrypted refuses plaintext, and one end marking it alone is how an HMI ends up
# showing NO CONTROLLER with nothing obviously wrong.
mfg-scada       114
mfg-order       115

# Alarms are PUSHED to an HMI rather than waiting for its poll (H3.1, H3.5), so an
# HMI binds this and keeps it bound -- a push that finds nobody listening is lost.
#
# NOTE: nothing sends to it yet. The controller raises and stores alarms, and an HMI
# picks them up on its next `mfg-scada` poll, so alarms work with up to one poll
# interval of latency. Registering the name is what lets the HMI bind it at all; the
# sender is still to be written.
mfg-alarm       116
